  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 339 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n \n- High confidence dry forecast through next Tuesday\n\nMoisture overviews in BUFKIT remain very dry into early next week.\nOutside of some high clouds today, the overviews in the GFS are\nvoid of RH values above 50-60 percent. Confidence in dry weather \nis also boosted by the fact that all 80 members of the GFS and \nECMWF ensembles show dry weather through Tuesday. Not much else to\nsay in this quiet period of weather other than the fact that the \npattern is somewhat blocked by the Middle Atlantic/Northeast \ncoastal low that is forming. This pattern block keeps the ridge\nover the Great Lakes almost anchored. Bottom line...dry weather \nfor the next 5 days.\n\n- Rain possible mid next week\n\nThere has been some discrepancy in the models in the medium range\nand that seems to be clearing up a little bit in the past 24\nhours. As yesterday's discussion noted, the GFS had trended\ntowards the ECMWF, and that remains to be the case today. The GFS\nhad a deep low moving through the Great Lakes on Wednesday, but\nconsensus is building somewhat towards more of a surface\ntrough/cold front moving through. Chances for rain begin Tuesday\nnight, but are higher on Wednesday into Wednesday night. The GFS\nis still hanging on to some semblance of a surface low albeit\nweaker and further south than last night. The surface low hangs\nthe front up a bit over Southwest Lower Michigan. The ECWMF is\nmore progressive. Bottom line is that we have rain chances mid\nnext week but the details are still a bit murky. \n\n- Near normal temperatures through the 7 day forecast\n\nHigh temperatures through the period look to range from the upper\n60s to the lower 70s which is very close to normals. With no\nmajor system moving through there will not be much fluctuation\nfrom day to day. Lows through Monday night will be near normal as\nwell, generally in the 40s",
